  Glad to help.  Nice to see someone thinking about overall gameplay
instead of focusing on details.

  One reason I like this change is it takes away one of the big
motivations to start consulting spoilers.  NetHack becomes more of a
play-n-go game where players learn by playing.  The latest
developments on NAO trend in that direction, with interface
enhancements to aid the player.

  Something else NetHack needs is another level of difficulty.  Maybe
two.  Right now there's Explore mode, save-scumming, and regular mode.
Explore mode is too easy early on and can wind up in irretrievable
situations.  Save-scumming can hurt a player's development.  Regular
mode's "die-n-learn" method is too brutal for real learning.  Hence
spoilers, which aren't really an improvement on save-scumming, imho.

  The pool of players for NetHack has changed, thanks to NAO.
Non-computer savvy people with Internet connections used to playing,
communicating and competing online.

  So a 'FunHack' fork.  Only fun relative to regular NetHack, of
course.  To serve its target community a server would be needed.
FunHack would have everything automatically fully identified.  It's
tough enough to figure out what things do without knowing what they
are when you use them.  One less reason to reach for the spoilers
every other turn.

  Instead of death prevention like Explore Mode, or permadeath like
regular mode, have a checkpoint system where death knocks the player
back to the previous checkpoint.  The checkpoint system is widely used
in the gaming universe.  It makes the player's life easier by removing
the need for managing save files.  It gives the developer some control
and a way to guide and communicate game goals with the player.

  Initially checkpoint at the start of the game and as each level is
exited.  Adjust checkpoint intervals from there.

  Something else to add would be jimrandomh's Duplicate NetHack patch
http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/6ecf537ed6b34b32/1edd6847fb5ea2b5#1edd6847fb5ea2b5

With the Duplicate NetHack patch the dungeon is the same for every
playthrough.  Important for checkpoints farther apart than every
level.

  Game seeds that produce dungeons that are particularly
newbie-friendly could be stored and used for that purpose.




